{
  "markdown_report": "**Recommendation:** retain the fixed 0.5 terminal/reconstruction objective and add a small, reversible optimizer-transfer audit. At 40% progress, this offers a feasible advance beyond gradient cosine without introducing adaptive weighting or another predictor.\n\n**Insights from the starting point.** (1) Equal branch coefficients do not imply equal optimization influence. Per-sequence means control sequence weighting, while target exposure and gradient norms still differ. (2) Serving parity passed all 67 cases/category in the latest supplied diagnostics. Missing SciPy nevertheless interrupted execution, so residual-patching results remain unavailable. (3) Electronics’ recent-deletion diagnostic hit@10 change was −0.125, with interval [−0.21875, −0.046875]. This motivates studying terminal prediction, but establishes neither objective conflict nor validation improvement.\n\n**Insights from related work.** (1) [Recency Sampling](https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.02643) identifies weak correspondence between reconstruction and next-item prediction. This motivates testing whether reconstruction updates help appended-mask prediction. (2) [Task Affinity Grouping](https://openreview.net/pdf?id=hqDb8d65Vfh) measures transfer using hypothetical updates and subsequent loss changes. Unlike cosine alone, this observes the consequences of a specified finite update. (3) [Krichene–Rendle](https://research.google/pubs/on-sampled-metrics-for-item-recommendation/) establish limitations of sampled recommendation metrics. Consequently, the pinned validation protocol remains the selection criterion, with conclusions restricted to that protocol.\n\nThree directions organize these observations: objective alignment, diagnostic reliability, and evaluation scope.\n\n| Direction | Existing measurement | Remaining gap | Proposed addition |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Alignment | Branch weighting; gradient cosine | Finite optimizer-step transfer | Reversible update audit |\n| Reliability | Sampler/export parity | Interrupted or misleading probe status | Independent probe execution |\n| Evaluation | Fixed sampled HR@10 | Generalization beyond candidates | Explicit scope and denominators |\n\n**Candidate ideas.** Scores are originality/potential/difficulty; higher difficulty means more implementation work.\n\n- **Optimizer-transfer audit: 5/7/5.** Originality: applies established affinity methods to BERT4Rec, but adds no new learning rule. Potential: tests local objective interaction, but may not predict eventual HR@10. Difficulty: reuses losses and optimizer, but requires exact restoration.\n- **Target-count-controlled reconstruction audit: 3/6/3.** Originality: isolates exposure, but uses standard subsampling. Potential: clarifies weighting interpretations, but leaves context differences. Difficulty: needs one target selection rule, but careful sampler accounting [Recency Sampling].\n- **Fault-validated diagnostic execution: 4/7/4.** Originality: adapts mutation testing, but remains infrastructure. Potential: exposes silent probe failures, but cannot establish recommendation gains. Difficulty: small temporary fixtures suffice, but clean-control coverage requires care [MDPMorph](https://valerio-terragni.github.io/assets/pdf/li-ase-2025.pdf).\n\n**Selected algorithm.** At one preregistered checkpoint, use disjoint public training-user prefixes for update and assessment. Compare reconstruction, terminal, and no-update conditions from identical model and optimizer state. Measure terminal-loss reduction, fixed-candidate margins, and parameter displacement; include one-target reconstruction as an exposure control.\n\n```text\nsnapshot model, optimizer, buffers, mode, RNG\nfor condition in [none, terminal, reconstruction, one_target_reconstruction]:\n    restore snapshot\n    apply zero or one declared optimizer step on update prefixes\n    measure terminal loss and margins on disjoint assessment prefixes\nrestore snapshot; verify exact restoration\n```\n\nBudget this audit before execution. Train exactly one BERT4Rec per category; preserve fixed mixture training, host validation, interfaces, and predictor consistency. Audit outcomes are descriptive and never trigger reweighting.",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Add a preregistered, reversible optimizer-transfer audit to the fixed 0.5 terminal/reconstruction BERT4Rec experiment. Measure whether one reconstruction update locally improves terminal prediction on disjoint public training prefixes, relative to terminal-update and no-update controls.",
    "motivation": "The supplied evidence supports serving consistency and conditional history sensitivity, but provides no measured objective-transfer result or validation gain. Equal branch coefficients and gradient cosine cannot establish the effect of a finite optimizer update. Task-affinity measurements provide a practical next diagnostic while keeping the trained model and deployment unchanged.",
    "implementation_notes": "Keep the corrected BERT4Rec architecture and its actual baseline loss, negative sampler, endpoint/window policy, and all-MASK corruption. Train the fixed mixture with exactly half of an even batch assigned to each branch, using per-sequence target means and L=0.5*L_terminal+0.5*L_reconstruction. Do not introduce adaptive weighting from audit results.\n\nPredeclare one checkpoint/update index, audit batch count, user-disjoint update and assessment sets, candidate construction, optimizer-step policy, numerical thresholds, and a small diagnostic compute allocation within the shared remaining 43,200 GPU-second budget. Budget availability is not supplied, so do not promise additional training arms. Each category trains exactly one independent model; audit conditions are temporary interventions on that model and never produce deployed checkpoints or fused scores.\n\nFor each paired audit batch, use identical frozen starting parameters, buffers, and optimizer state across no-update, terminal-update, reconstruction-update, and one-target reconstruction conditions. Reproduce the current optimizer's parameter groups, moments, learning rate, weight decay, and clipping policy. Use evaluation mode with autograd enabled and float32 to control dropout, explicitly reporting that this differs from stochastic training. Assess terminal loss with the same declared loss and fixed negative samples across conditions. Also report target-minus-tenth-negative margin on fixed 100-item diagnostic candidate sets where eligible; this is a training-prefix diagnostic, not host validation. Use user-disjoint assessment prefixes without consulting later interactions to determine candidate eligibility. Record losses before and after, raw loss differences, Z=1-L_after/L_before when the baseline loss exceeds a preregistered threshold, per-block parameter displacement norms, target counts, and elapsed GPU time. Return unavailable values with reasons for invalid denominators or insufficient candidates. Local transfer remains conditional on checkpoint, prefixes, optimizer state, and step size.\n\nRestore parameters, buffers, optimizer, model mode, gradients, and Python/NumPy/Torch/CUDA RNG state in finally blocks. Assert exact restoration of model and optimizer state before resuming training; restoration failure must stop continuation. Store only diagnostic measurements, never an alternative fitted predictor. Count tied parameters once in block summaries.\n\nRepair the missing SciPy dependency through dependency-free implementations of the specific required statistics, or mark only the dependent probe unavailable. Execute probes independently and preserve completed measurements. Report actual residual-patching failure causes rather than stale sampler/parity gate messages. Retain DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis), all ModelView interfaces, family-specific sample_batch, and B x L loader tensors.\n\nKeep all four category routes, preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, at most 200 epochs and exactly 200 batches per epoch, single-GPU execution, DATA_DIR=os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], and trial-local artifacts. Serving retains maxlen-1 history items, appends reserved MASK, and reads its actual final nonpadding position, never count-1. Preserve masked-target training without shifted-target leakage. Each category loads exactly one checkpoint; synchronize checkpoint configuration and predictor writer. Preserve five designated real training users/category receiving full-catalog unseen-item recommendations. Use only available public histories, titles, descriptions, and timestamps. Select solely using the unchanged four-category macro validation HR@10; leave held-out evaluation to the host.",
    "pseudocode": "train one corrected BERT4Rec per category using the fixed half mixture\nat the preregistered audit update:\n    snapshot model, buffers, optimizer, gradients, mode, and all RNG states\n    select budgeted user-disjoint update and assessment training prefixes\n    freeze assessment masks, negatives, and eligible diagnostic candidates\n    try:\n        for paired audit batch:\n            for condition in [NO_UPDATE, TERMINAL, RECONSTRUCTION, ONE_TARGET_RECONSTRUCTION]:\n                restore identical snapshot\n                model.eval()\n                if condition != NO_UPDATE:\n                    construct condition-specific all-MASK supervision\n                    loss = mean(per_sequence_mean(unchanged_target_loss))\n                    apply one step with the declared current optimizer policy\n                measure terminal assessment loss and fixed-candidate margins\n                record target exposure and parameter displacement\n        summarize paired local transfer with explicit denominators and costs\n    finally:\n        restore every saved state\n        assert exact model and optimizer restoration\nresume unchanged training\nsave one category checkpoint with matching embedded predictor\nsubmit through unchanged host validation",
    "originality": {
      "score": 5,
      "positive": "Connects optimizer-aware task-affinity measurement to serving-aligned BERT4Rec objectives with disjoint assessment prefixes and an exposure control.",
      "negative": "Hypothetical-update transfer is established; this is a controlled diagnostic adaptation rather than a new optimization algorithm."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 7,
      "positive": "Could distinguish locally helpful reconstruction updates from harmful ones and motivate subsequent falsifiable training hypotheses.",
      "negative": "One-step transfer may be checkpoint-specific and need not predict convergence or validation HR@10."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 5,
      "positive": "Reuses the existing encoder, sampler, losses, and optimizer without changing deployed inference.",
      "negative": "Exact optimizer restoration, disjoint-prefix sampling, controlled randomness, and exception-safe continuation require careful implementation."
    }
  },
  "related_work": [
    {
      "title": "Recency Sampling for BERT4Rec",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.02643",
      "contributions": [
        "Identifies weak correspondence between masked reconstruction and next-item prediction, motivating direct objective-transfer measurement."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not establish that the proposed fixed mixture or audit improves this benchmark."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Task Affinity Grouping",
      "link": "https://openreview.net/pdf?id=hqDb8d65Vfh",
      "contributions": [
        "Measures task interaction through hypothetical updates and relative changes in another task's loss."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Local loss transfer does not guarantee long-term recommendation ranking gains."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "MDPMorph",
      "link": "https://valerio-terragni.github.io/assets/pdf/li-ase-2025.pdf",
      "contributions": [
        "Combines metamorphic relations with model mutations to assess fault detection."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Supplies no measured detection rates for this BERT4Rec diagnostic adapter."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "On Sampled Metrics for Item Recommendation",
      "link": "https://research.google/pubs/on-sampled-metrics-for-item-recommendation/",
      "contributions": [
        "Establishes that sampled recommendation metrics can yield inconsistent model comparisons."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not justify changing the pinned host evaluator or extrapolating its scores to full-catalog performance."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
